    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Principal-Business-Development-Officer_R-68485-2": {
      "title": "Principal Business Development Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nQueensland Alliance for Agriculture and Food Innovation.   Full-time, fixed-term position for up to 3 years.   Base salary will be in the range $106,709 to $116,198 + 17% super (HEW Level 7).   Based at our St Lucia location .     About This Opportunity   The Queensland Alliance for Agriculture and Food Innovation is seeking a purposeful and highly motivated Principal Business Development Officer to help develop strategic research partnerships with industry and investors and progress major initiatives across the Institute.     Working in a collaborative environment alongside the Head of Business Development, research leaders, researchers and professional teams, you will connect partner needs and market opportunities with QAAFI’s research capabilities.     Through strategic influence, trusted relationships and operational excellence, you will help diversify QAAFI’s research investment portfolio and translate innovative research into meaningful outcomes for agriculture and food industries.     Key responsibilities will include , but not limited to :   Coordinate the development of large-scale, multi-stakeholder research initiatives, including business cases, concept papers, briefing materials, investment proposals, workshops and stakeholder forums.   Develop productive relationships with industry stakeholders, investors, government agencies and research partners, while identifying opportunities for strategic partnerships, co-investment and commissioned research.   Work with researchers and research leaders to scope and progress funding opportunities, project concepts and collaborative research proposals that align with partner requirements and University and QAAFI frameworks.   Identify research and intellectual property with commercial potential and collaborate with UniQuest , researchers and external stakeholders to support translation, commercialisation, industry uptake and research impact.     About You   A degree with four years of subsequent relevant experience in business development, industry partnership development, commercialisation, intellectual property management or a related field, or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training and experience.   Relevant experience within the agriculture and food sector, a university, research organisation, Cooperative Research Centre, Research and Development Corporation or innovation ecosystem.   Demonstrated experience developing and maintaining productive investment relationships with stakeholders across industry, government or the research sector.   Experience coordinating complex initiatives or engagement activities involving multiple stakeholders and organisational boundaries.   Demonstrated ability to identify , develop and progress business development opportunities, strategic partnerships, co-investment arrangements or commissioned research.   Sound understanding of the Australian research, innovation, commercialisation and research funding environment, with familiarity in research commercialisation processes and intellectual property management frameworks being highly advantageous .   High-level interpersonal, facilitation, negotiation, customer engagement and relationship management skills, with the ability to build trust and communicate effectively with diverse stakeholders.   Excellent written and verbal communication skills, sound judgement and the ability to prepare compelling reports, proposals and briefing materials while independently managing competing priorities.     About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available.     Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.     Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Oliver Frith o.frith@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line.     When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.     Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.     You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.     We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.     Applications close Sunday 20 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68485 )."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Professor-and-Head-of-School--School-of-Mathematics-and-Physics_R-67591-1": {
      "title": "Professor and Head of School, School of Mathematics and Physics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-28\nPosted: Posted 12 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Science | School of Mathematics and Physics Full-time (100%), fixed-term appointment for five years with an underlying continuing Teaching and Research appointment at Academic Level E Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity Join a community where curiosity, discovery and impact come together. As Professor and Head of School, School of Mathematics and Physics, you will provide visionary academic and organisational leadership for one of Australia’s leading mathematics and physics communities. This is a rare opportunity to shape the School’s strategic direction, champion excellence in teaching, research and engagement, and inspire a diverse and high-performing academic community. Working alongside talented academic and professional staff, you will foster an environment where innovation thrives, partnerships flourish, and future generations of scientists, educators and researchers are empowered to create meaningful change. The School of Mathematics and Physics is a vibrant, multidisciplinary community with strengths across mathematics, statistics, physics and astrophysics. Home to more than 120 academic staff, dedicated professional teams and a substantial postgraduate research cohort, the School is recognised for world-class teaching, impactful research and strong engagement with industry, government and community partners. As a collaborative, ambitious and globally connected leader, you will help shape the next chapter of excellence while contributing to UQ’s vision of knowledge leadership for a better world. For more information about this opportunity, please click here to view the information pack . About You You are an accomplished and inspiring academic leader with a PhD or equivalent qualification in a discipline aligned to the School and an international reputation for excellence. Your outstanding record spans high-quality teaching, influential research, successful research funding, publication performance and effective Higher Degree by Research supervision. You bring the credibility and confidence required to lead a large and diverse academic community while maintaining your own academic standing. You are a visionary, inclusive and collaborative leader who excels at building relationships, shaping organisational culture and delivering strategic outcomes. With highly developed interpersonal, communication and people leadership skills, you have demonstrated success leading complex organisations, driving innovation, managing resources responsibly and creating environments where staff and students thrive. You are committed to fostering excellence, supporting career development, advancing equity and inclusion, strengthening partnerships, and contributing to positive outcomes for communities locally, nationally and globally. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. At the core of our teaching remains our students, and their experience with us sets a foundation for success far beyond graduation. UQ has made a commitment to making education opportunities available for all Queenslanders, regardless of personal, financial, or geographical barriers. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. Interested? If you’d like to have a confidential discussion, please contact Caitlin Savage, Principal Consultant, Executive Recruitment via executiverecruitment@uq.edu.au When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter, and responses to the selection criteria. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications and criminal history checks. Academic appointments will also be subject to verification of the highest academic qualification from the conferring institution. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here . We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing executiverecruitment@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close: Sunday 11 October at 11.00pm AEST (R-67591)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Principal-Advisor--Aboriginal-and-Torres-Strait-Islander-Services---Collections--Identified-s25-_R-68052-1": {
      "title": "Principal Advisor, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Services & Collections (Identified s25)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-27\nPosted: Posted 13 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nUQ Library – Join a library with a strong commitment to reconciliation, cultural inclusion and a diverse workforce. This position is open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates only, as a genuine occupational requirement due to the cultural expertise, community engagement and leadership required to support Indigenous knowledge, collections and initiatives across UQ. Full-time fixed-term position until September 2028 (planned leave coverage). Base salary will be in the range $106,709 - $116,198 plus 17% superannuation (HEW Level 7). Based at our Vibrant St Lucia Campus. Acknowledgement of Country  The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.  About This Opportunity    As Principal Advisor, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Services & Collections, you will help shape how Indigenous knowledge, collections and cultural materials are respected, protected and shared across one of Australia's leading universities. Working closely with stakeholders across UQ's libraries, academic communities and professional teams, you will provide high-level specialist advice, facilitate engagement activities, contribute to the implementation of Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property frameworks, and help ensure culturally appropriate approaches are embedded in practice. You will play an important role in supporting the stewardship, protection and future use of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander knowledge and collections across UQ, helping advance teaching, learning and research outcomes while strengthening the University's commitment to reconciliation and cultural respect. Applicants are advised that collection materials they may interact with in this role may contain images, voices, and videos of deceased persons. Items within the collection may also include language, descriptions, terms, or annotations that are culturally sensitive and reflective of the attitudes and perspectives of the period in which they were created, and which may be considered inappropriate today. Key Responsibilities will include: Lead and support strategic Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander engagement initiatives across the University. Provide expert advice on the care, assessment and management of Indigenous knowledge and culturally significant collection materials. Build strong partnerships with internal and external stakeholders to support teaching, learning and research outcomes. Contribute to the implementation and continuous improvement of Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property frameworks and practices. Mentor and support team members through knowledge-sharing, capability development and culturally informed guidance. Promote culturally safe, respectful and inclusive practices that recognise and value Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander knowledges and perspectives. For further information, please click here to view the information booklet . About You     The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld). The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person.  Demonstrated knowledge of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander cultures, communities and protocols, and the ability to apply this knowledge in a professional environment. Experience providing specialist advice, influencing stakeholders and contributing to strategic initiatives or projects. Strong relationship-building skills and the ability to work collaboratively across diverse teams and disciplines. Excellent communication skills, with the ability to engage, advise, educate and influence a broad range of audiences. Experience supporting culturally safe practices and delivering positive outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and communities. A commitment to mentoring, collaboration and knowledge-sharing At UQ, we value diverse experiences and perspectives. We understand that no candidate is likely to tick every box, and we're interested in hearing from people who can bring their unique skills, lived experience, knowledge and passion to the role. If you feel you can contribute to our work and help advance positive outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and communities, we encourage you to apply. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you will work alongside bright minds from around the world, united by a shared commitment to creating a better world. Excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and staff are supported through a wide range of benefits, including up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, salary packaging, and cultural leave.  UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by our Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. You will be supported to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, and your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Lesley Acres on l.acres@library.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.      Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Sunday 20 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68052)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Rockhampton-Hospital/Senior-Community-Engagement-Officer_R-67975-1": {
      "title": "Senior Community Engagement Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-26\nPosted: Posted 14 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nUQ Poche Centre for Indigenous Health. Part-time (80%), fixed-term position for up to 2 years. Base salary will be in the range $120,317 to $134,893 + 17% super (HEW Level 8). This position is open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates only, as a genuine occupational requirement\\, due to its focus on culturally safe Indigenous health research and community engagement. Based in Rockhampton, Central Queensland. Acknowledgement of Country  The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.  About This Opportunity Create meaningful change in Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health through a purposeful clinical research role with the UQ Poche Centre for Indigenous Health. Working within a multidisciplinary, community-led program focused on reducing sexually transmissible infections, you will apply your clinical knowledge, research expertise and collaborative approach to deliver culturally safe, high-quality research. Drawing on your understanding of Good Clinical Practice, research ethics, governance requirements and relevant privacy and confidentiality obligations, you will help ensure the integrity and impact of research activities. Success in this role will rely on your ability to build respectful relationships, communicate effectively in sensitive and complex environments, and manage competing priorities with strong organisational and administrative capability. Key responsibilities will include: Provide clinically informed advice and research support to strengthen service delivery, data quality and research outcomes within a multidisciplinary health program. Coordinate local study activities with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, including site establishment, ethics, research governance, authorisations and reporting requirements. Build trusted partnerships with community-controlled health organisations, government agencies, health services, clinicians, research participants and university stakeholders. Apply sound judgement and initiative to identify emerging issues, manage competing priorities and support ethical, culturally safe and effective research delivery. For further information, please click here to view the information booklet. About You The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld). The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person. Demonstrated experience working with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples in health, clinical or research settings, with an understanding of culturally safe and community-led practice. Advanced knowledge of sexually transmissible infections and significant experience working with people affected by STIs, their families, clinicians and health service providers. Experience in clinical research, including data collection, interpretation, analysis and the maintenance of accurate research records. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Dr Gary Jones . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications and criminal history checks, eligibility to be credentialled within Queensland Health facilities, mandatory immunisations and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Wednesday 16th September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-67975)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Principal-Advisor--Indigenous-Employment--S25---Identified-_R-68051-1": {
      "title": "Principal Advisor, Indigenous Employment (S25 - Identified)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-20\nPosted: Posted 20 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOffice of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Indigenous Engagement) . Make a positive contribution to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ employment and careers. Full-time, permanent position with Base salary in the range of $106,709 - $116,198 + 17% super (HEW Level 7). Identified s25: Open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates only, as a genuine occupational requirement. Based at our Vibrant St Lucia Campus. Acknowledgement of Country    The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society.   About This Opportunity    Join UQ's Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Indigenous Engagement) as the Principal Advisor, Indigenous Employment and help shape a workforce where Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples can thrive. In this influential role, you'll lead programs and initiatives that create meaningful career opportunities, strengthen Indigenous representation across UQ and support our commitment to reconciliation, inclusion and lasting change. Working closely with communities, leaders and stakeholders across the University, you'll help drive key Indigenous employment initiatives, including UQ's graduate and professional entry pathway programs. You'll play a pivotal role with supporting ways to increase representation of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples within UQ’s workforce. You’ll perform an active advocacy role which fosters cultural understanding and inclusive workplaces through staff development, engagement activities and services contributing to UQ’s reconciliation initiatives. Key Responsibilities will include: Lead and enhance UQ's Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Graduate and Professional Entry Pathways Programs. Drive employment initiatives that increase Indigenous representation across the University. Partner with leaders and stakeholders to support Indigenous attraction, recruitment and retention strategies. Coordinate professional development, mentoring, training and engagement opportunities for Indigenous staff. Build strong relationships with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities and contribute to UQ's Reconciliation Action Plan commitments.   For further information, please click here to view the information booklet.   About You    The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld). The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person.  Your experience and strengths will include: A degree in an aligned discipline such as human resources, business, or project management and or at least 4 years equivalent demonstrated professional experience and training. Demonstrated understanding of the histories and distinct cultures of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and an ability work with these communities. Demonstrated high level written communication and interpersonal skills including the ability to build relationships and engage with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. Demonstrated experience in managing projects, from inception to delivery, including the setting of performance targets, measures, reporting of outcomes and achievements. Experience with providing general advice on policies and procedures, enterprise agreements, administrative support, and governance within a large organisation. Experience contributing to the development, coordination and implementation of recruitment and advancement strategies and practices. Ability to prioritise own workload, work independently and as part of a team to meet deadlines. Desirable An existing connection to Indigenous communities. Previous experience in a similar role within higher education or large organisation. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you will work alongside bright minds from around the world, united by a shared commitment to creating a better world. Excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued, and staff are supported through a wide range of benefits, including up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% superannuation, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, salary packaging, and cultural leave.  UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by our Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. You will be supported to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, and your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Chris Levinge at c.levinge@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.     Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Friday 11th Sept at 11.00pm AEST (R-68051)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Expression-of-Interest---UQ-Temps-Administration-Opportunities_R-41821-4": {
      "title": "Expression of Interest - UQ Temps Administration Opportunities",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-06-12\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nCasual\n\nUQ Temps: Connects skilled professionals with a variety of casual opportunities across University of Queensland Opportunities : Various casual opportunities Casual Hourly Rate: Minimum $51 - $67 per hour + super Locations: St Lucia Campus, Herston Campus, Dutton Park Campus Explore Casual Opportunities with UQ Temps Are you looking for flexible, short-term work where you can contribute your skills in a dynamic and collaborative environment? UQ Temps connects talented professionals with a diverse range of casual opportunities across the University of Queensland. These roles provide a unique opportunity to gain experience across various teams while building your professional network. We are currently seeking expressions of interest for a range of casual opportunities, including: General Administration Executive Assistant support Student and Academic Administration Research Administration Junior Web Development About This Opportunity Working as part of the UQ Temps casual talent pool, you will support teams across the University to deliver high-quality administrative and operational outcomes. These roles are varied and fast-paced, offering exposure to different functions and stakeholders across UQ’s vibrant community. Your responsibilities may vary depending on the role but can include: Managing calendars, coordinating meetings and arranging travel Supporting committees and meetings, including agenda preparation and minute-taking Maintaining administrative systems and managing competing priorities Conducting research and data analysis to support projects and reporting Liaising with internal and external stakeholders in a professional manner Assisting with website updates and development using Drupal (for relevant roles) About You We are looking for adaptable and motivated professionals who bring: Demonstrated experience in administration, business support or executive assistance Strong interpersonal and written communication skills High levels of discretion and professionalism when handling sensitive information Excellent organisational and problem-solving abilities A proactive mindset with the ability to thrive in fast-paced environments Flexibility and a willingness to support a range of teams and functions A sound understanding of administrative systems and processes Experience with Drupal or web development (for web-focused opportunities) About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world. Everyone here has a role to play. As a member of our professional staff cohort, you will be actively involved in working towards our vision of a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits such as: Access to exclusive internal opportunities Ability to quickly build organisational knowledge UQ benefits such as vaccination programs, EAP, fitness passport, and more Networking opportunities In addition, the following mandatory requirements apply: Work Rights:  You must maintain your own unrestricted work rights in Australia whilst you work for UQ. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.  Background Checks:  All final applicants for this position may be asked to consent to a criminal record check. Please note that people with criminal records are not automatically barred from applying for this position. Each application will be considered on its merits.    Questions?   For more information about this opportunity and application queries, please contact uqtemps@uq.edu.au  stating the job reference number in the subject line.   For more information about UQ Temps, please visit UQ Temps - Candidates . Want to Apply?   All applicants must upload the following documents in order for your application to be considered: Cover letter addressing the ‘About You’ section, including preferred working location and role.   Resume  Please note that you will be asked to add all documents into the one upload box labelled ‘resume’, which is step one of the application form. Our strength as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion , fostering an environment that mirrors our wider community. We're committed to attracting, retaining, and promoting diverse talent, particularly from Australia’s First Nations Peoples. If you require an alternative method to submit your application due to accessibility needs or personal circumstances, please contact  talent@uq.edu.au .     Other Information    UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges which have impacted and/or continue to impact their career trajectory. Candidates who don’t meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunities when assessing suitability for the role.   Applications will be reviewed as received. Candidates may be interviewed prior to the job closing date. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible. The University of Queensland reserves the right to close this application process early."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/UQ-Indigenous-Graduate-Program---Identified--s105-_R-52069-2": {
      "title": "Indigenous Graduate Program - Identified (s105)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-01-13\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nOffice of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Indigenous Engagement (ODVCIE) These positions are only open to Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. We have multiple full-time fixed-term graduate positions for up to two years with a salary of $83,776 (HEW 5) plus a generous super allowance of up to 17%. A fantastic opportunity for recent graduates! The UQ Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Graduate Program is an opportunity to accelerate your career and receive hands-on experience at a world-leading university. Based at our picturesque and conveniently located St Lucia campus Kickstart your career. Shape the future. Stay connected to community. Are you a current student about to graduate, or have you graduated within the past three years? The Indigenous Graduate Program is your pathway to professional growth, cultural empowerment, and meaningful impact. About This Opportunity  The Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Graduate Program accelerates careers by building on your bachelor’s degree with tailored practical skill development. This program is designed to equip you with the essential skills to succeed in your chosen career, providing a unique opportunity to leverage your potential into a fulfilling professional journey. Why Join? Tailored career development: Structured training, mentoring, and leadership pathways designed for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander graduates. Cultural safety and belonging: Work in an environment that values your identity, knowledge, and lived experience. Competitive salary & benefits: $83,776 (HEW 5) plus 17% superannuation, with flexible work arrangements. Community connection: Opportunities to contribute to UQ’s Indigenous Engagement Strategy and strengthen ties with community. If you’ve graduated within the last three years, this program is designed to help you take the next step in your career journey. Please click here to watch Edwin Dyson (graduate of the Vice-Chancellor's Indigenous Graduate Program) talk about his experience with the program . For further information, please click here to view the information booklet. Why Work at UQ? The University of Queensland is recognised as one of Australia’s leading institutions, offering staff access to world‑class facilities, flexible work arrangements, and a vibrant campus community. As part of UQ, you’ll benefit from professional development opportunities, mentoring, and a supportive environment that values diversity and inclusion. UQ’s Reconciliation Action Plan (RAP) and Indigenous Employment Strategy guide our commitment to creating culturally safe workplaces and increasing Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander representation across the university. By joining this program, you’ll be part of a community that actively works towards reconciliation, respects cultural knowledge, and supports Indigenous staff to thrive. About You  The University of Queensland considers that the filling of this position constitutes an equal opportunity measure under s105 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991(Qld) and under section 8(1) of the Racial Discrimination Act 1975 (Cth). The positions are therefore only open to Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person. You may be required to complete background checks including, criminal history and education checks. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. Share With Your Community If this opportunity isn’t the right fit for you, we warmly encourage you to share it with your family, friends, and community networks. Helping spread the word ensures that more Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander graduates can access this pathway to professional growth and contribute to UQ’s vision for reconciliation and inclusion. About UQ At UQ, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are supported to fill their cultural responsibilities, with access to 8 additional days (pro-rata) of paid Cultural Leave annually. Indigenous employees are encouraged to access the UQ Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network with a variety of significant events held throughout the year.  As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world.   Everyone here has a role to play. As a member of our professional staff cohort, you will be actively involved in working towards our vision of a better world. By supporting the academic endeavour across teaching, research, and the student life, you will have the opportunity to contribute to activities that have a lasting impact on our community.  Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available, such as:  26 weeks paid parental leave or 14 weeks paid primary caregiver leave, 17% superannuation contributions, 17.5% annual leave loading. Access to flexible working arrangements including hybrid working options, flexible start/finish times, purchased leave, and a condensed fortnight. Health and wellness discounts – fitness passport access, free yearly flu vaccinations, discounted health insurance, and access to our Employee Assistance Program for staff and their immediate family. Cheap parking, On campus childcare and Salary packaging options. Want to Apply?   All applicants must upload the following documents in order for your application to be considered: Cover letter addressing how your degree and area of interest align with your preferred career pathway at UQ.   Resume  Questions? Please reach out to the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment team on  indigenous_employment@uq.edu.au to discuss. Applications will be reviewed as they are received, so we encourage you to submit yours as soon as possible to begin the process. Job Reference Number - R-52069 UQ is committed to a fair, equitable and inclusive selection process, which recognises that some applicants may face additional barriers and challenges which have impacted and/or continue to impact their career trajectory. Candidates who don’t meet all criteria are encouraged to apply and demonstrate their potential. The selection panel considers both potential and performance relative to opportunities when assessing suitability for the role.  We know one of our strengths as an institution lies in our diverse colleagues. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion , fostering an environment that mirrors our wider community. We're committed to attracting, retaining, and promoting diverse talent. Reach out to talent@uq.edu.au for accessibility support or adjustments.  If you are a current employee (including casual staff and HDR scholars) or hold an unpaid/affiliate appointment, please login to your staff Workday account and visit the internal careers board to apply for this opportunity. Please do NOT apply via the external job board."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Advisor--Safer-Communities_R-65355-1": {
      "title": "Advisor, Safer Communities (St Lucia Campus)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nStudent Support & Wellbeing Services . Play a key role in early support, helping identify needs and connect people to appropriate services  Full-time, Permanent with a Base salary in the range of $95,361 - $102,302 + 17% super (HEW Level 6)  Based at our  Vibrant St Lucia Campus. About This Opportunity    Ready to make a real impact? As an Advisor, Safer Communities, you’ll play a vital frontline role in supporting safe, respectful and inclusive environments across UQ. Acting as a trusted first point of contact, you’ll respond to disclosures of gender-based violence, behavioural risks and other concerns with empathy and professionalism. You’ll work within a highly collaborative team to deliver trauma-informed, person-centred support during sensitive and complex situations helping ensure coordinated, timely responses that prioritise safety, wellbeing and dignity for our students and staff.   Key responsibilities will include:    Deliver student‑centred support using trauma‑informed and culturally responsive approaches that promote wellbeing, safety, and inclusion. Coordinate and deliver impactful programs and initiatives that support student welfare, mental health, and harm prevention. Provide clear, accurate guidance on support services, pathways, and reporting options to students and stakeholders. Apply policies, frameworks, and legislation confidently, tailoring approaches to meet individual and situational needs. Manage intake, triage, and case coordination with professionalism, maintaining accurate records and confidentiality. This role includes the opportunity and expectation to travel to UQ campuses at Dutton Park, Gatton and Herston while being predominantly based at St Lucia. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of  benefits and rewards  are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.  About You    A degree in a relevant discipline, or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build rapport and work effectively with diverse stakeholders Demonstrated judgement in applying policies, procedures and legislation, with the ability to adapt processes to meet outcomes Experience coordinating programs, projects or initiatives, including planning, timelines and delivery Highly organised, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines in a service-focused environment Strong analytical skills, with the ability to interpret data and support decision-making Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Kirsty Jackman on kirsty.jackman@uq.edu.au.  For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume  and  cover letter  summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Sunday 13 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-65355).  Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Contract-Auditor_R-67760-1": {
      "title": "Contract Auditor (Facilities Maintenance Services)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nProperty & Facilities Division, Campus Operations. Full-time, continuing position. Base salary will be in the range $97,269 - $104,349 + 17% super (HEW Level 6). Based at our St Lucia campus. About This Opportunity Join a collaborative and purpose-driven team supporting one of Australia’s leading universities. As Contract Auditor, you will play a critical role in strengthening governance, compliance, and contractor performance assurance across UQ’s diverse infrastructure and service contracts. Working within Campus Operations, you will contribute to a systematic auditing function that supports operational excellence, transparency, value for money, and continuous improvement across a complex contractor portfolio. This is a field-based role where you will work closely with contractors, service delivery teams, and asset management professionals to help create meaningful change across UQ’s campuses. Key responsibilities will include: Audit contractor performance across infrastructure services including mechanical, electrical, hydraulic, civil, and transportation contracts. Audit contractor performance across soft services including cleaning, pest management, and waste services to ensure compliance with contractual requirements and service specifications. Review contractor invoices, assess compliance against agreed contract rates, and identify value-for-money opportunities. Develop audit reports, communicate findings, monitor non-conformances, and contribute to ongoing contractor performance improvement initiatives. About You Completion of a building-related degree with subsequent relevant experience, extensive specialist expertise in a related field, or an equivalent combination of trade experience and education. Demonstrated understanding of contract management principles, contractor auditing frameworks, and compliance assurance practices. Sound knowledge of mechanical, electrical, or plumbing services within large and complex facilities, including technical, scientific, or laboratory environments. Experience reviewing contractor performance, undertaking audits, and contributing to contract management and negotiation activities. Strong analytical capability with a high level of attention to detail and commitment to data accuracy and completeness. Proven proficiency using a broad range of systems and software, including facilities management and data management applications. Excellent organisation, project management, and stakeholder engagement skills with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively while exercising initiative, sound judgement, and autonomy in a complex operational environment. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of benefits and rewards are available. Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on-campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging. At UQ, we are creating change together. The Property & Facilities Division supports a vibrant, world-class campus environment and plays a vital role in enabling UQ’s teaching, research, and community outcomes. Recent UQ initiatives continue to showcase the University's commitment to innovative facilities, infrastructure, and sustainability outcomes, reinforcing the University's vision of delivering meaningful impact for future generations. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Franchoan Smit at f.smit@uq.edu.au For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, this may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion. We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Monday 14th September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-67760)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Lecturer---Law_R-68307-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Law",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-02\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSchool of Law within Faculty of Business, Economics and Law Two Full-time continuing positions Base salary will be in the range $119,463 - $141,545 + 17% superannuation (Academic Level B) Based at our vibrant St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  Join one of Australia’s leading law schools and contribute to a visionary, collaborative and inclusive academic community committed to creating change together. This teaching and research opportunity will see you deliver outstanding learning experiences across core law courses, including the prescribed areas of knowledge required for admission to legal practice, while advancing an impactful research agenda with national and international reach. Working alongside talented colleagues and engaged students, you will be challenged, inspired and supported to shape the next generation of legal professionals and contribute to meaningful legal scholarship, law reform and policy development. Key responsibilities will include:  Teaching: Develop, coordinate and deliver courses (including those that comprise the Priestley 11 prescribed areas of knowledge required for admission to legal practice), engage in curriculum design, assess students, provide academic counselling, and adhere to University teaching rules.   Research: Establish a research program, collaborate on research projects, seek and manage research funding, publish in reputable domestic and international journals, utilize best-practice research methodologies, and participate in project discussions.   Supervision and Researcher Development: Supervise students, recruit and manage employees, provide performance feedback and training, resolve conflicts, and ensure correct remuneration and benefits.   Citizenship and Service: Develop partnerships, demonstrate leadership through mentoring, engage in internal service roles and committees, perform administrative functions, provide support to colleagues, and uphold University values. This is a Teaching & Research position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . For further information, please click here to view the information booklet. About You  Completion of a doctoral qualification in Law. Demonstrated experience teaching at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level, with evidence of effective and engaging teaching practice. A willingness and ability to develop, coordinate and deliver courses that comprise the Priestley 11 prescribed areas of knowledge required for admission to legal practice. Experience developing innovative curriculum, assessment and learning materials that enhance the student experience and support academic success. A demonstrated record of high-quality research outputs published in respected national and international outlets. Evidence of, or strong potential for, securing competitive research funding and establishing a sustained research program. Demonstrated capacity to supervise or contribute to the supervision of Higher Degree by Research students. Ability to build productive relationships with industry, government, professional bodies and the broader legal community. Strong communication, interpersonal and leadership capabilities, with a commitment to collaboration, mentorship and academic citizenship. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research-funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career-progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Rick Bigwood, Sir Gerard Brennan Chair in Law and Head of School via email r.bigwood@law.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resum é , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. To apply, you must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet all criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Tuesday 29 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68307). Please note applications will be reviewed as received. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow_R-68514-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Geochemistry or Geochronology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-07\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSchool of the Environment. Full-time, fixed-term position through to 22 February 2029. Base salary will be in the range $85,373 to $113,660 plus 17% super, (Academic Level A). Based at our St Lucia Campus . About This Opportunity. Join a visionary and collaborative research team investigating the history of climate and coral mortality in the Coral Sea Marine Park. Based within the Radiogenic Isotope Facility in UQ’s School of the Environment, you will contribute to the ARC Discovery Project, “Reconstructing climate and coral mortality in the Coral Sea Marine Park”, led by Professor Jian-xin Zhao, Dr Nicole Leonard and Professor Gregg Webb. Working alongside project investigators, laboratory specialists and international collaborators, you will use advanced geochemical and geochronological techniques to generate new insights into coral reef history, while building your research profile in a supportive environment equipped for high-impact discovery. Key responsibilities will include, but not limited to: Research: Produce high-quality research outputs in marine geochemistry and geochronology, palaeoecology and palaeoclimate reconstruction, including publications in high-impact peer-reviewed journals. Research : Prepare and analyse coral death assemblage samples, identify coral to genus level, undertake ion exchange column chemistry, perform high-precision U-Th isotope measurements/age dating and apply trace element geochemistry to reconstruction of past sea-surface temperature and other environmental parameters. Supervision and Researcher Development : Contribute to the effective co-supervision of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, providing guidance in experimental design, laboratory techniques and data interpretation, while helping to foster a collaborative and safe laboratory environment. Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate UQ values through purposeful collaboration, participation in priority activities, internal and external service, and the development of relationships with industry, government, professional bodies and the wider community. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . About You Completion, or near completion, of a PhD in a relevant field such as marine geology, geochemistry, geochronology or a closely related discipline. An emerging research profile in geochemistry, geochronology, palaeoecology, palaeoclimate reconstruction or a relevant discipline. Demonstrated knowledge of, and practical experience in, a HEPA-filtered ultra-clean isotope geochemistry laboratory environment. Experience in sample preparation, ion exchange column chemistry, low-level trace element geochemistry and/or high-precision radiogenic isotope analysis. Demonstrated experience in U-Th geochronology, MC-ICP-MS instrumentation or relevant radiogenic isotope analytical techniques. Demonstrated ability to interpret geochemical and isotope data, preferably with relevance to palaeoclimatic and paleoenvironmental reconstructions. A publication record commensurate with experience, together with excellent analytical, problem-solving, collaboration and communication skills. Experience in coral identification and the use of ArcGIS or similar spatial interpretation software is desirable. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. Your research will connect with a wider community helping to deepen understanding of coral ecosystems and their resilience. Read about UQ research showing how longer-distance coral larval movement can strengthen coral populations , an example of the collaborative and globally relevant marine science being advanced across the University. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Jianxin Zhao j.zhao@uq.edu.au  or Dr Nicole Leonard n.leonard@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number below in the subject line. When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume, cover letter, and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Tuesday 15 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68514)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Toowong/Research-Fellow---Indigenous-Urban-Health--Identified-s25-_R-68292-2": {
      "title": "Research Fellow - Indigenous Urban Health (Identified s25)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-03\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPoche Centre for Indigenous Health Full-time, fixed-term position for up to 3 years. Base salary will be in the range $119,462.94 to $141,545 + 17% super (Academic Level B). This position is open to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates only, as a genuine occupational requirement due to the requirement to have strong community engagement and First Nations research responsibilities. Based at our Toowong location. Acknowledgement of Country  The University of Queensland (UQ) acknowledges the Traditional Owners and their custodianship of the lands on which UQ operates. We pay our respects to their Ancestors and their descendants, who continue cultural and spiritual connections to Country. We recognise their valuable contributions to Australian and global society. About This Opportunity  This is an exciting opportunity to join Professor James Ward and the team at the Poche Centre for Indigenous Health as part of a landmark ARC Laureate Program exploring Indigenous urban health. Working within a collaborative and community-led research environment, you will help deliver a program of research that creates meaningful change for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples living in Australian cities. We're looking for a passionate and community-minded researcher who is driven by making a real difference. You will be a relationship builder who can work respectfully across community, academic, government and industry settings, with a genuine commitment to conducting research with and for First Nations communities. This is a unique opportunity to contribute to impactful research, grow your leadership and supervision experience, and help shape research that informs policy, practice and positive social change. About This Opportunity  Key responsibilities will include:  Research: Develop an independent and collaborative research program across priority areas related to the health and well-being of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples living in cities including cultural identity, visibility, pride, and belonging; access to services and opportunities for economic participation and empowerment; Indigenous knowledges in urban planning and sustainability; and culturally appropriate data systems and infrastructure. Research translation and impact: Produce quality research outputs, contribute to or lead funding applications, and translate findings into accessible knowledge products, policy influence and practical outcomes for First Nations communities and other research users. Supervision and Researcher Development: Contribute to the effective supervision and development of Honours and Higher Degree by Research students, including Indigenous HDR students, while facilitating engagement and capability-building opportunities. Citizenship and Service: Build purposeful partnerships with First Nations communities, government, industry, professional bodies and the wider community, while contributing through mentoring, collaboration, internal service and active participation in priority activities. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . For further information, please click here to view the information booklet. About You  The University of Queensland considers that to perform this role, it is essential that the person is an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander person. It is therefore a genuine occupational requirement under section 25 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld). The position is therefore only open to Australian Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander people. The successful candidate will be required to provide evidence to confirm that they are an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person. Completion of a PhD in a relevant discipline, which may include public health, psychology, business, urban planning or another field that supports the objectives of the research program. Knowledge of and experience in applying Indigenous research methodologies, and/or substantial experience working with and for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities. A developing research profile, including quality publications or other research outputs and experience presenting or sharing findings with academic, community or policy audiences. Experience contributing to research funding applications and supporting the effective management and delivery of externally funded projects. A developing record of supervising Honours or Higher Degree by Research students, with the capability and commitment to support Indigenous HDR students. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will join a Group of Eight university recognised for excellence in teaching, research and engagement. UQ offers a supportive and rewarding academic environment, with benefits including recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible and hybrid working arrangements , access to exclusive internal‑only vacancies, cultural leave, and genuine career progression through the academic promotions process. You will also have opportunities to connect with vibrant staff networks, including the Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Staff Network, fostering a strong sense of community and belonging. UQ is committed to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples’ learning, discovery and engagement, and to playing a leading role in reconciliation in Australia. Guided by the University’s Strategic Plan, Stretch Reconciliation Action Plan and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Employment Strategy, we work in partnership with communities to embed reconciliation across our operations. Your cultural identity will be respected, valued and embraced, and you will be supported to contribute to an organisation that champions Indigenous voices. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Associate Professor Jaimee Stuart . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarizing how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Sunday 4 October 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68292). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the week commencing Monday12 October."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/Herston-Campus/Mayne-Professor-and-Head--Mayne-Academy-of-Paediatrics_R-68257-1": {
      "title": "Mayne Professor and Head, Mayne Academy of Paediatrics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nMedical School Part-time (30%–50% FTE), five-year fixed-term position at Clinical Academic Level E. Based at our Herston Campus About This Opportunity This is an exceptional opportunity for a visionary clinical academic to lead the Mayne Academy of Paediatrics and advance the discipline’s contribution to education, research and engagement. Working within UQ’s Medical School, you will shape a collaborative and ambitious environment that supports staff to excel, strengthens the Academy’s international profile, and helps prepare medical students to improve child and adolescent health. You will provide academic leadership across curriculum, assessment, research and researcher development, working closely with Clinical Schools and Units, the Office of the Director, MD Curriculum & Assessment, and partners across the University. You will also build purposeful relationships with specialist medical colleges, health services, government, hospitals and the community, connecting expertise and diverse perspectives to create meaningful change in paediatrics.   For more information about this opportunity, please view the information pack.   About You You will be a widely respected leader in paediatrics, with a medical qualification, eligibility for registration as a medical practitioner in Australia, and a specialist qualification recognised by the Royal Australasian College of Physicians. You will also hold a PhD or equivalent in a relevant disciplinary area, or bring an appropriate combination of professional and academic service relevant to the field.   You will bring an outstanding record of academic achievement, including high-impact research, successful researcher supervision, funding, knowledge translation and international engagement. An empathetic and collaborative leader, you will demonstrate excellence in medical education, curriculum and assessment, alongside the ability to build effective teams, develop academic capability, and establish productive relationships with academic, clinical, government, industry and professional partners. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. At the core of our teaching remains our students, and their experience with us sets a foundation for success far beyond graduation. UQ has made a commitment to making education opportunities available for all Queenslanders, regardless of personal, financial, or geographical barriers.   As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development.   Interested? If you’d like to have a confidential discussion, please contact Professor Craig Munns, Dean, Medical School at med.medicaldean@uq.edu.au or Caitlin Savage, Principal Advisor, Talent Acquisition , via talent@uq.edu.au .   When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume and cover letter summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.   Other Information Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration with AHPRA, criminal history checks and mandatory immunisations. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   Please note that the successful candidate’s appointment may be progressed as a conjoint appointment or secondment through Queensland Health. We’re dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criterion but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity.   We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623.   Applications close Tuesday 29 September 2026 at 11.00 pm AEST (R-68257)"
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Service-Support-Coordinator_R-67788-1": {
      "title": "Service Support Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAcademic Skills & Student Central team . Empower students to succeed by helping them access the support, guidance and services they need throughout their university journey. Full-time, permanent with Base salary will be in the range $97,268.85 - $104,348.98 + 17% super (HEW Level 6)  Based at our  Vibrant St Lucia Campus. About This Opportunity    We have a fantastic opportunity for an experienced coordinator to make a meaningful difference in the lives of students at UQ. As the Coordinator, Service Support, you will play a key role in ensuring the effective day-to-day operation of a complex Student Support and Wellbeing service. Leading a team responsible for first-line enquiries, triage, and administrative support, you will help create a responsive, student-centred service that enables students to access the right support at the right time. Working within a trauma-informed framework, you will oversee service processes and triage pathways, ensuring they are efficient, consistent, and aligned with best practice. You will support the wellbeing and effectiveness of the administrative team by implementing processes that promote appropriate professional boundaries, manage psychosocial risks, and enable sustainable service delivery. Key responsibilities will include:   Coordinate the day-to-day operations of the Service Support team, ensuring the delivery of timely, accurate, and professional administrative services. Oversee the management of high-volume first-line enquiries and triage processes, ensuring students are connected with appropriate services and supports. Providing person-centred, trauma-informed support, advice and referrals that promote student wellbeing, safety, equity and inclusion. Responding to sensitive and escalated student enquiries with empathy, sound judgement and a solutions-focused approach. Lead, support, coach, and develop administrative staff, fostering a collaborative, professional, and high-performing team culture.  Maintain accurate records and ensure compliance with relevant legislation, policy, privacy, and information management requirements. About You     You will thrive in a responsive, fast-paced environment where every day is different. With experience coordinating administrative and client-facing support services, you combine empathy and sensitivity with sound judgement, professionalism, and a commitment to student and staff wellbeing. An undergraduate qualification with significant relevant experience, extensive experience in student support, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience supporting people from diverse backgrounds, including culturally and linguistically diverse and underrepresented communities. High-level interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build rapport and maintain respectful, productive stakeholder relationships. Strong organisational and time management skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities accurately and meet deadlines. The ability to support staff wellbeing through effective processes, clear boundaries, and management of psychosocial risks. Advanced capability with Microsoft Office applications and confidence working with digital records and service systems. About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of  benefits and rewards  are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Catherine Fitzgerald on c.fitzgerald3@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume  and  cover letter  summarising how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.  Please limit your cover letter to a maximum of two pages.    Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, criminal history checks and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Tuesday 15 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-67788) - Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Lecturer---Law_R-68322-1": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Law",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSchool of Law within Faculty of Business, Economics and Law Full-time fixed term position for up to 2 years. Base salary will be in the range $119,463 – $141,545 + 17% superannuation (Academic Level B) Based at our vibrant St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity Join a collaborative, inclusive and high-performing academic community committed to creating change through outstanding legal education. This teaching-focused opportunity will contribute to the delivery of undergraduate core law courses, including the prescribed areas of knowledge required for admission to legal practice, while supporting student success, pedagogical innovation and educational leadership across the School of Law. Working alongside passionate colleagues, you will help shape the next generation of legal professionals through contemporary learning experiences that inspire, challenge and reward students. Key responsibilities will include: Teaching: Teach compulsory and elective law courses across undergraduate and postgraduate programs, coordinate courses, develop innovative curriculum and assessment materials, and enhance student learning and engagement through evidence-based teaching practices. Research and/or Teaching Practice and Impact: Contribute to the School's culture of educational excellence through leadership in teaching practice and educational innovation, mentor and support colleagues, and supervise undergraduate and postgraduate student research projects. Citizenship and Service: Engage in citizenship, service and engagement activities that support the School, Faculty and University, while contributing to a collaborative and inclusive academic community. This is a Teaching Focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s  Criteria for Academic Performance .  For further information, please click here to view the information booklet. About You A doctoral qualification in law that has either been conferred, is under examination, or is in the final stages of completion. Demonstrated experience teaching and supporting student learning within undergraduate and/or postgraduate legal education. A willingness and ability to develop, coordinate and deliver courses that comprise the Priestley 11 prescribed areas of knowledge required for admission to legal practice. Evidence of developing innovative curriculum, learning materials and assessment practices that enhance student outcomes. A strong commitment to teaching excellence, supported by teaching evaluations, peer recognition, awards and/or other indicators of impact. Demonstrated capability to contribute to educational leadership, pedagogical innovation and the broader culture of teaching excellence. Experience supervising or supporting undergraduate and/or postgraduate student research projects. The ability to establish productive relationships with colleagues, industry, government, professional bodies and the wider legal community. Excellent communication, interpersonal and collaboration skills, with a commitment to inclusive and respectful workplace practices. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant-writing support, greater research-funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, flexible working arrangements, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career-progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Rick Bigwood, Sir Gerard Brennan Chair in Law and Head of School via email r.bigwood@law.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resum é , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, qualifications, professional registration and criminal history checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. To apply, you must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment. Employer-sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment. We are dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet all criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We are also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Tuesday 29 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68322). Please note applications will be reviewed as received. We encourage candidates to apply as soon as possible."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Senior-Officer--Reporting-and-Evaluations_R-66261-1": {
      "title": "Senior Officer, Reporting and Evaluations",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nJoin the Widening Participation team Full-time, fixed-term opportunity through to December 2027 Salary will be in the range of $97,268.85 - $104,348.98 + 17% super (HEW Level 6) Based at our Vibrant St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity    An exciting fixed-term opportunity has become available within our Widening Participation team, following internal career progression. Step into a role with real purpose as a Reporting and Evaluations Officer, where you’ll collect, analyse and interpret data to shape and enhance programs that support students from underrepresented backgrounds driving meaningful change and better outcomes across UQ.   Key responsibilities will include:    Designing, administering and managing surveys and other data collection activities to gather meaningful insights on program reach, participant experiences and outcomes. Collecting, analysing and interpreting complex quantitative and qualitative datasets to support reporting, evaluations, briefings and strategic decisions -making. Preparing high-quality reports for internal stakeholders and external  requirements including government requirements. Coordinating program evaluations and contributing to the design of a robust evaluation framework Identifying trends, insights and opportunities to improve program design, delivery and impact Collaborating with stakeholders across the University to align data collection and reporting with strategic priorities Supporting the continuous improvement of data collection methodologies, survey instruments and evaluation processes to ensure data quality and usability. About You     You’re a detail-oriented analyst who enjoys translating data into clear, actionable insights. You bring a strong understanding of equity programs and thrive in a collaborative environment. You will demonstrate: Relevant qualifications and experience in data analysis, evaluation, or a related field Proven ability to analyse dynamic datasets and generate meaningful insights using tools such as Excel or Business Objects Strong written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to present insights clearly to diverse audiences Excellent stakeholder engagement skills and a collaborative approach to working across teams Strong organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines An understanding of equity-focused initiatives within higher education (highly regarded) Hold (or be eligible to obtain) a Queensland Government Blue Card About UQ   As part of the UQ community, you’ll have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, working together to create a better world. Join a community where excellence is at the core of our culture, contributions are valued and a range of  benefits and rewards  are available.   Enjoy up to 26 weeks paid parental and primary care leave, 17% super, 17.5% leave loading, flexible and hybrid working options, health and wellbeing programs, a heavily discounted Study for Staff program, on‑campus childcare, affordable parking, and salary packaging.  Interested?   For more information about this opportunity, please contact Corinne Francken on c.francken@uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at  talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.   When you apply, please ensure you upload a  resume  and  cover letter  summarizing how your background aligns to the ‘About You’ section.  Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted.      Other Information    Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia, criminal history checks and Blue Card checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements.   You must maintain unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this appointment to apply. Employer sponsored work rights are not available for this appointment.   We're dedicated to  equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ, and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing  talent@uq.edu.au  or calling +61 7 3365 2623.  Applications close Tuesday 15 Sept 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-66261)."
    },
    "https://uq.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/uqcareers/job/St-Lucia-Campus/Postdoctoral-Research-Fellow-in-Power-System-Stability_R-68501": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Power System Stability",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-01\nPosted: Posted 8 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFaculty of Engineering, Architecture and Information Technology / School of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science Full-time fixed-term position through to end of September 2029 Base salary will be in the range $85,372.89 - $113,659.72 + 17% super (Academic Level A) Based at our St Lucia Campus About This Opportunity  Join a visionary and collaborative team helping to shape Australia’s net-zero energy future. We are seeking a Research Associate to contribute to the ARC Discovery Project A Reliable and Secure Net-Zero Power System for Australia , while also supporting the Clean Innovation Researcher Networks (ICIRN) project during the first six months of the appointment. Working alongside a team of seven researchers in one of Australia’s largest power systems research groups, you will apply your expertise in power electronics and controller hardware experimentation to develop, validate and deploy innovative solutions that enhance the reliability, affordability and security of renewable energy systems. This is an exciting opportunity to work with world-class facilities, build an emerging research profile, and contribute to meaningful change through high-impact research. Key responsibilities will include:  Research: Lead and support research activities in power electronics, power system stability, hardware-in-the-loop testing, real-time simulation, oscillation source identification, and data-driven modelling methods for renewable energy integration. Research Outputs and Funding: Publish high-quality research outcomes, contribute to milestone reports, collaborate on competitive research funding applications, and develop an emerging international research profile. Supervision and Researcher Development: Provide support to Honours, BE/ME and Higher Degree Research students while contributing to a positive, inclusive and high-performing research environment. Citizenship and Service: Demonstrate collaborative leadership, contribute to School and University activities, build industry and community partnerships, and uphold UQ values through professional service and engagement. This is a research focused position. Further information can be found by viewing UQ’s Criteria for Academic Performance . About You  Completion or near completion of a PhD in Electrical Engineering or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated expertise in power electronics, renewable energy systems, or power system stability. Experience in hardware experimentation, controller implementation, and hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) validation. Experience with data-driven modelling techniques, real-time digital simulation, or electromagnetic transient (EMT) modelling using commercial tools. Evidence of quality research outputs, including publications in reputable peer-reviewed journals and presentations at leading conferences. Demonstrated ability to work effectively within a multidisciplinary and collaborative research team. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to prepare technical reports and research documentation. Emerging evidence of contributing to student supervision, academic service, industry engagement, or collaborative research initiatives. About UQ As part of the UQ community, you will have the opportunity to work alongside the brightest minds, who have joined us from all over the world, and within an environment where interdisciplinary collaborations are encouraged. As part of our commitment to excellence in research and professional practice in academic contexts, we are proud to provide our staff with access to world-class facilities and equipment, grant writing support, greater research funding opportunities, and other forms of staff support and development. The greater benefits of joining the UQ community are broad: from being part of a Group of Eight university, to recognition of prior service with other Australian universities, up to 26 weeks of paid parental leave, 17.5% annual leave loading, access to exclusive internal-only vacancies, and genuine career progression opportunities via the academic promotions process. Interested? For more information about this opportunity, please contact Professor Tapan Saha at saha@eecs.uq.edu.au . For application inquiries, please reach out to the Talent Acquisition team at talent@uq.edu.au , stating the job reference number (below) in the subject line.  When you apply, please ensure you upload a resume , cover letter , and responses to the ‘About You’ section. Please note that applications received via email will not be accepted. Other Information   Pre-employment checks may include: verification of the right to work in Australia and qualification checks. This may also include checks relating to gender-based violence matters or other integrity and conduct requirements. Relocating from interstate or overseas? We may support you with obtaining employer-sponsored work rights and a relocation support package, however upfront costs will still be expected to be covered. You can find out more about life in Australia’s Sunshine State here . We're dedicated to equity, diversity, and inclusion . We recognise that career pathways and opportunities differ and encourage applications from candidates who may not meet every criteria but can demonstrate their potential relative to opportunity. We’re also happy to support any accessibility needs throughout the recruitment process. Just let us know how we can help by emailing talent@uq.edu.au or calling +61 7 3365 2623. Applications close Wednesday 30 September 2026 at 11.00pm AEST (R-68501). Please note that interviews have been tentatively scheduled for the first two weeks of October 2026."
    },
